[R0589] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Technology and Society Magazine | type=JOUR
TITLE: The Unprecedented Surge in Generative AI: Empirical Analysis of Trusted and Malicious Large Language Models (LLMs)
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Training; Data models; Chatbots; Large language models; Ethics; Computer crime; Computational modeling; Codes; Trusted computing; Malware
ABSTRACT: Trusted large language models (LLMs) inherit ethical guidelines to prevent generating harmful content, whereas malicious LLMs are engineered to enable the generation of unethical and toxic responses. Both trusted and malicious LLMs use guardrails in differential contexts per the requirements of the developers and attackers, respectively. We explore the multifaceted world of guardrails implementation in LLMs by conducting an empirical analysis to assess the effectiveness of guardrails using prompts. Our results revealed that guardrails deployed in the trusted LLMs could be bypassed using prompt manipulation techniques such as “pretend” and “persist” to generate harmful content. In addition, we also discovered that malicious LLMs still deploy weak guardrails to evade detection by generating human-like content. This empirical analysis provides insights into the design of the malicious and trusted LLMs. We also propose recommendations to defend against prompt manipulation and guardrails bypass while designing LLMs.

[R0590] year=2026 | venue=2026 12th International Conference on Big Data and Information Analytics (BigDIA) | type=CONF
TITLE: A Systematic Survey of Prompt Injection Attacks and Defenses in LLM-Integrated Applications and Agentic Systems
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Tools; Security; Large language models; Printing; Memory; Electronic mail; Toxicology; Fluid flow; Conferences; Prompt Injection; LLM Security; Information-Flow Control; Agentic Systems; Defense Taxonomy; Undecidability; Safe Utility Ratio
ABSTRACT: Prompt injection (PI), listed as LLM01:2025 by OWASP, converts a data-representation problem into an execution-control problem: untrusted content is processed alongside privileged instructions, while large language models lack a native instruction/data boundary. Unlike jailbreaking, which primarily targets content-safety policies, PI targets application control flow and can induce unauthorized tool calls, information disclosure, or persistent-state corruption. This Systematization of Knowledge (SoK) studies PI from a provenance and information-flow perspective. It contributes (1) a three-tier attack taxonomy organized by contamination vector, attacker capability, and persistence; (2) a critical comparison of four defense lines and their trust assumptions; and (3) a cross-benchmark reporting audit together with Safe Utility Ratio (SUR), a metric for exposing security gains obtained by sacrificing task utility. We formalize PI as low-integrity data influencing high-integrity control flow, separate this integrity failure from subsequent confidentiality leakage, and identify evidence gaps in adaptive and stateful-agent evaluation.

[R0591] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE IAS Petroleum and Chemical Industry Technical Conference (PCIC) | type=CONF
TITLE: Comprehensive Cybersecurity for Variable Frequency Drives Standards, Practices, and Implementation Revision Jan 2025
KEYWORDS: Industries; Protocols; Industrial control; Security information and event management; Variable speed drives; Safety; Security; Standards; Uninterruptible power systems; Industrial Internet of Things; IIoT - Industrial Internet of Things; VFDs - Variable Frequency Drives; Al - Artificial Intelligence; ICS - Industrial Control Systems; IACS - Industrial Automation and Control System; SDL - Secure Development Lifecycle; DoS - Denial-of-Service; UPS - Uninterruptible Power Supply; MISRA - Motor Industry Software Reliability Association; LLMs - Large Language Models; ZTNA - Zero Trust Network Access; SOC - Security Operations Center; SIEM - Security Information and Event Management
ABSTRACT: Variable Frequency Drives - being the muscles of an industrial operation - store, process and transmit critical data. In this age of increased connectivity and Industry 4.0, various embedded devices that build up the operational technology network are getting connected to the enterprise network. As the proverbial air-gap has nearly evaporated, the industry needs more than disciplined network segmentation to keep the factories protected against cyber-attacks. A malicious attacker could potentially exploit flaws in an unsecure device in the infrastructure and compromise the system, and in effect impact the operations and even compromise the functional safety of the system.This paper explores the how and what of building secure variable frequency drives and in extension best practices for developing secure components, that enables us to ensure an attack resilient manufacturing ecosystem. We delve into factors to consider for building a sustainable and successfully repeatable program including developing security requirements for the devices, implementing and validating these into device in compliance to industry recognized 62443-4-1 [3] and 4-2 [4] standards.

[R0592] year=2024 | venue=IEEE Access | type=JOUR
TITLE: LLM for SoC Security: A Paradigm Shift
KEYWORDS: Security; Task analysis; Fuzzing; Cognition; Complexity theory; Databases; Hardware security; System-on-chip; Large language models; Hardware security; SoC security verification; hardware vulnerability; large language model
ABSTRACT: As the ubiquity and complexity of system-on-chip (SoC) designs increase across electronic devices, incorporating security into an SoC design flow poses significant challenges. Existing security solutions are inadequate to effectively verify modern SoC designs due to their limitations in scalability, comprehensiveness, and adaptability. On the other hand, large language models (LLMs) are celebrated for their remarkable success in language understanding, advanced reasoning, and program synthesis tasks. Recognizing an opportunity, our research explores leveraging the emergent capabilities of generative pre-trained transformers (GPTs) to address the existing gaps in SoC security, aiming for a more efficient, scalable, and adaptable methodology. By integrating LLMs into the SoC security verification paradigm, we open a new frontier of possibilities and challenges to ensure the security of increasingly complex SoCs. This paper offers an in-depth analysis of existing works, presents practical case studies, and demonstrates comprehensive experiments. We also present the achievements, prospects, and challenges of employing LLM in different SoC security verification tasks.

[R0593] year=2023 | venue=2023 IEEE/ACM Symposium on Edge Computing (SEC) | type=CONF
TITLE: Net-GPT: A LLM-Empowered Man-in-the-Middle Chatbot for Unmanned Aerial Vehicle
KEYWORDS: Protocols; Computational modeling; Predictive models; Autonomous aerial vehicles; Chatbots; Computer security; Edge computing; Man-in-the-Middle (MITM); Large Language Model; System Security; Cyber Attack
ABSTRACT: In the dynamic realm of AI, integrating Large Language Models (LLMs) with security systems reshape cybersecurity. LLMs bolster defense against cyber threats but also introduce risks, aiding adver-saries in generating malicious content, discovering vulnerabilities, and distorting perceptions. This paper presents Net-GPT, an LLM-empowered offensive chatbot that understands network protocols and launches Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAV)-based Man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks against a hijack communication between UAV and Ground Control Stations (GCS). Facilitated by an edge server equipped with finely tuned LLMs, Net-GPT crafts mimicked network packets between UAV and GCS. Leveraging the adaptabil-ity of popular LLMs, Net-GPT produces context-aligned network packets. We fine-tune and assess Net-GPT's LLM-based efficacy, showing its impressive generative accuracy: 95.3% for Llama-2-13B and 94.1% for Llama-2-7B. Smaller LLMs, such as Distil-GPT-2, reach 77.9% predictive capability of Llama-2-7B but are 47x faster. Cost-efficiency tests highlight model quality's impact on accuracy while fine-tuning data quantity enhances predictability on spe-cific metrics. It holds great potential to be used in edge-computing environments with amplified computing capability.

[R0594] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Access | type=JOUR
TITLE: Beekeeper: Accelerating Honeypot Analysis With LLM-Driven Feedback
KEYWORDS: Measurement; Botnet; Penetration testing; Malware; Automation; Servers; Large language models; Costs; Testing; System recovery; Automation; beekeeper; feedback; honeypots; LLM
ABSTRACT: Honeypots are decoy resources intended to entice adversaries and collect threat intelligence in the process. The amount and quality of the collected insights strongly correlate with the honeypot’s credibility to the adversary. However, the development of medium to high interaction honeypots, so, environments that offer at minimum a shell to the attacker, is laborious and complex. Additionally, getting feedback on a honeypot is often expensive and time-consuming, slowing down development and discouraging investment into honeypots. Therefore, we propose Beekeeper: a modular framework that combines static tests, known attack sequences, and automated, large language model based querying to investigate medium to high interaction honeypots. Afterward, the results are analyzed to provide feedback on the current state of the system and recommendations on how to improve key characteristics of the honeypot. To demonstrate the framework’s functionalities, we deploy Beekeeper with two medium and one high interaction systems and highlight how feedback and recommendations change after an initial set of improvements is implemented for each honeypot.

[R0595] year=2025 | venue=2025 5th International Conference on Trends in Material Science and Inventive Materials (ICTMIM) | type=CONF
TITLE: Real-Time IoT Cybersecurity using Machine Learning-based AI Threat Detection System to Train Generative Robots
KEYWORDS: Training; Privacy; Protocols; Federated learning; Real-time systems; Threat assessment; Internet of Things; Computer security; Protection; Robots; IoT cybersecurity; Machine learning; Federated learning; Generative AI; AI-driven threat detection; CNN-LSTM; Adversarial training; Real-time security; Edge computing
ABSTRACT: Existing security protocols encounter major cybersecurity difficulties because the online devices' growing popularity continues to spread across networks. These security protocols are ineffective because they do not properly handle current cyber threats. The main goal of this study involves developing enhanced IoT cybersecurity through the development of a threat detection system which brings together adversarial training and deep learning models (CNN-LSTM) and Federated Learning (FL). The system enables distributed Internet of Things devices to work on security model development through Federated Learning while maintaining total privacy of their information. Security procedures controlled by generative artificial intelligence robots alongside real-time attack protection functions decrease security response durations. Through its Federated CNN-LSTM model the system upholds a 1.2% false positive rate alongside a 98.3% accuracy evaluation and 160 milliseconds of exact threat tracking time. The designed system sustains a minimal occurrence of incorrect alarm activations. The developed system provides real-time security for the Internet of Things framework because it enables adaptive protection systems while preserving user privacy in current IoT settings.

[R0596] year=2025 | venue=2025 International Research Conference on Smart Computing and Systems Engineering (SCSE) | type=CONF
TITLE: Integrating Large Language Models for Automated Vulnerability Scanning and Reporting in Network Hosts
KEYWORDS: Automation; Accuracy; Grounding; Large language models; Retrieval augmented generation; Manuals; Computer architecture; Systems engineering and theory; Computer security; Systematic literature review; cybersecurity; exploiting; llm; scanning; vulnerability
ABSTRACT: This research explores integrating Large Language Models (LLMs) like GPT-4 and Claude 3.5 into cybersecurity vulnerability scanning to enhance automation and effectiveness. Current tools’ reliance on manual updates and human expertise is highlighted. A literature review identified effective modular architectures and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems for grounding LLMs with cybersecurity knowledge.A Proof of Concept (PoC) tool, developed in Python and tested on the Metasploitable system, evaluated three LLM implementations: GPT-4 Omni, GPT-4 Omni with RAG, and Claude 3.5 Sonnet. The results showed GPT-4 Omni outperformed Claude 3.5, with RAG significantly improving performance. The tool achieved 80% accuracy in identifying and resolving vulnerabilities.The study underscores the potential of LLMs to revolutionize vulnerability scanning, making advanced cybersecurity more accessible and effective. Future work should address limitations, enable interactive sessions, create new exploits, and tackle more complex challenges.

[R0597] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=JOUR
TITLE: Automating Cyber Risk Assessment With Public LLMs: An Expert-Validated Framework and Comparative Analysis
KEYWORDS: Risk management; Measurement; Computer security; Engines; Cognition; Surveys; Cyber threat intelligence; Closed box; ISO Standards; Generative AI; Cyber risk assessment; large language models (LLMs); generative AI; rank order centroid (ROC); automated risk scoring; human–AI comparison
ABSTRACT: Traditional cyber risk assessment methodologies face a critical dilemma: they are either quantitative yet static and context-agnostic (e.g., CVSS), or context-aware yet highly labor-intensive and subjective (e.g., NIST SP 800-30). Consequently, organizations struggle to scale risk assessment to match the pace of evolving threats. This paper presents an automated, context-aware risk assessment framework that leverages the reasoning capabilities of publicly available Large Language Models (LLMs) to operationalize expert knowledge. Rather than positioning the LLM as the final decision-maker, the framework decouples semantic interpretation from risk scoring authority through a transparent, deterministic Dynamic Metric Engine. Unlike complex closed box machine learning models, our approach anchors the AI’s reasoning to this expert-validated metric schema, with weights derived using the Rank Order Centroid (ROC) method from a survey of 101 cybersecurity professionals. We evaluated the framework through a comparative study involving 15 diverse real-world vulnerability scenarios ( $C_{1}-C_{15}$ ) and three supplementary sensitivity stress tests ( $C_{16}-C_{18}$ ). The validation scenarios were independently assessed by a cohort of ten senior human experts and two state-of-the-art LLM agents (GPT-4o and Gemini 2.0 Flash). The results show that the LLM-driven agents achieve scoring consistency closely aligned with the human median (Pearson $r$ ranging from 0.9390 to 0.9717, Spearman $\rho $ from 0.8472 to 0.9276) against a highly reliable expert baseline (Cronbach’s $\alpha =0.996$ ), while reducing the assessment cycle time by more than $100\times $ (averaging under 4 seconds per case vs. a human average of 6 minutes). Furthermore, a dedicated context sensitivity analysis ( $C_{13}-C_{15}$ ) indicates that the framework adapts risk scores based on organizational context (e.g., SME vs. Critical Infrastructure) for identical technical vulnerabilities. Importantly, the system is designed not merely to replicate expert intuition, but to enforce bounded, policy-consistent risk evaluation under predefined governance constraints. Overall, these findings suggest that commercially available LLMs, when constrained by expert-validated metric schemas, can support reproducible, transparent, and real-time risk assessments.

[R0598] year=2025 | venue=2025 3rd World Conference on Communication & Computing (WCONF) | type=CONF
TITLE: Real Time Trade Settlement for the US Market
KEYWORDS: Distributed ledger; Large language models; Atomic layer deposition; Scalability; Smart contracts; Systems architecture; Throughput; Real-time systems; Stability analysis; Interoperability; Real-Time Settlement(T+0); Distributed Ledger Technology for Finance; Agentic AI; Smart Contract Atomic Settlement
ABSTRACT: The US market recently moved to a T+1 settlement cycle, which is quite a big step forward, but the real goal here is achieving real time settlement - basically T + 0 or instant settlement - with the ultimate goal of achieving real-time (T+0) settlement, which could significantly enhance the efficiency and stability of U.S. financial markets. This paper proposes a complete solution for instant trade settlement in the US market. Our approach involves using technologies like Distributed Ledger Technology (DLT) and Blockchain and combining them with intelligent automation using Agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) for their analytical power. The system being detailed here is built around a private, permissioned blockchain setup. This approach gives us immutable record keeping and atomic settlement capabilities through smart contracts. What this means is that tokenized securities and cash can be transferred instantaneously. When it comes to Agentic AI, this technology is being looked at to handle various tasks autonomously. Tasks like pre trade validation, managing real time liquidity, optimizing collateral, and dealing with post trade exceptions. These capabilities collectively aim to reduce both operational and counterparty risks. As for LLMs, these will work as intelligent interfaces for monitoring and reporting. They will handle regulatory compliance automation and improve market insights using natural language processing. This whole integrated framework being proposed should help drastically cut down counterparty risk, boost liquidity, reduce operational costs, and make things more transparent and auditable. This paper also tackles some key challenges - regulatory alignment, interoperability issues, scalability concerns, and cybersecurity aspects. This provides a realistic roadmap for where U.S. financial market infrastructure might head in the future. This work introduces a comprehensive framework that brings together permissioned distributed ledger technology, autonomous AI agents, and large language models to streamline regulatory processes. The result is a real-time (T+0) settlement system specifically designed to meet the demands of institutional trading in the U.S. financial markets.

[R0599] year=2025 | venue=2025 Cyber Awareness and Research Symposium (CARS) | type=CONF
TITLE: Modern SaaS Security Challenges and Defense Strategies
KEYWORDS: Industries; Technological innovation; Generative AI; Large language models; Software as a service; Organizations; Vectors; Security; Automobiles; Software as a Security (Saas); Multi-Tenancy; Cloud Platform; Artificial Intelligence; Large Language Model (LLM)
ABSTRACT: Adoption of Software as a Service (SaaS) solution has increased significantly in the past few years across all domains of software implementation. While SaaS has played critical role in innovation and simplified implementation of features at an accelerated pace in the software industry, it has also introduced new security challenges and attack vectors. This industry track paper presents a focused perspective on the evolving SaaS security landscape in the software industry. The paper breaks the root causes of SaaS security challenges into four different categories, outlines practical security challenges faced by organizations in implementation and the different defense strategies that should be taken to address the SaaS security threats.

[R0600] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Network | type=JOUR
TITLE: Agentic Vertical Federated Learning for Trustworthy Network Security
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Cognition; Cognitive systems; Radio access networks; Regional area networks; Planing; Retrieval augmented generation; 5G mobile communication; Large language models; Architecture
ABSTRACT: The transition to disaggregated 5G and 6G architectures has rendered centralized security models obsolete, necessitating a shift toward decentralized, autonomous defense. While Vertical Federated Learning (VFL) offers a promising path for collaborative detection across the RAN, Edge, and Core without exposing sensitive data, it introduces a critical “trust gap.” Network operators remain hesitant to authorize autonomous mitigation actions driven by “black box” neural networks that lack transparency and accountability. This article proposes a unified framework that bridges this gap by integrating Agentic AI, VFL, and Explainable AI (XAI). We introduce a distributed architecture where agents detect attacks using privacy-preserving VFL, but crucially, leverage a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) engine to reason before acting. By grounding AI decisions in NIST guidelines and operational Service Level Agreements (SLAs), the system transforms opaque alerts into transparent, evidence-backed mitigation plans. We demonstrate that this approach eliminates AI hallucinations and enables “surgical” responses—such as rate-limiting at the RAN or flow filtering at the Edge—that are fully traceable, policy-compliant, and trustworthy.

[R0601] year=2025 | venue=2025 3rd International Conference on Sustainable Computing and Smart Systems (ICSCSS) | type=CONF
TITLE: Intelligent Dark Web Monitoring: Threat Prediction using NLP and LLM Techniques
KEYWORDS: Dark Web; Electronic mail; Internet; Internet of Things; Communication systems; Industrial Internet of Things; Overlay networks; Protocols; Cloud computing; HTTP; Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI); Cybersecurity; Dark Web; DistilBERT; Large Language Models (LLMs); Metadata Enrichment; Natural Language Processing (NLP); Onion; Semantic Analysis; Sentence Transformers; SMTP; Threat Classification; Threat Prediction; Web Scraping
ABSTRACT: Dark web is a critical but long-overlooked source of Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI), where various actors usually engage in illegal activities, such as malware dissemination, zero-day exploit trade, and credential compromises. However, the high volume and unstructured nature of dark web data pose formidable challenges in efficient and substantial threat discovery and assessment. This work proposes a web scraping-based, automated Dark Web Intelligence System with Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Large Language Models (LLMs) to obtain actionable CTI. The system relies on Beautiful Soup(BS4) for spidering of .onion sites, Sentence Transformers for semantic threat typing, and a high-accuracy DistilBERT model. Threat metadata is enriched with severity scores, probable impact, and suggested mitigation steps, and stored in a centralized database for retrieval through a secure admin interface. A real-time automated dashboard provides URL scanning, interactive reporting, and trend analysis, with threat reporting sent at regular intervals. Empirical testing of the system shows a 72% accuracy level in threat prediction, which reflects the efficiency of the system in real-time extraction of CTI. The method provides a viable and intelligent solution to proactive cybersecurity defines against oncoming threats from the dark web.

[R0602] year=2025 | venue=2025 Conference on Information Communications Technology and Society (ICTAS) | type=CONF
TITLE: Large Language Models Adaptation for Web Applications Attacks Detection
KEYWORDS: Adaptation models; Accuracy; Large language models; Text categorization; Bidirectional control; Transformers; Encoding; Service-oriented architecture; Computer security; Payloads; Fine-tuning; Large Language Model; Web application attacks; Detection
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) represent a major advance in the field of deep learning. Their ability to understand long-term dependencies between words in a sentence has completely revolutionized natural language processing. Based on the architecture of transformers, LLMs are trained to solve common linguistic problems, such as text classification, question answering, text generation, document summarization, etc. LLMs can be pre-trained and then fine-tuned to perform tasks in specific domains. In the field of cybersecurity, we have proposed in our previous work, a LLM-based approach for web applications vulnerabilities detection. In this paper, we implement our approach, which uses adaptation techniques such as fine-tuning to detect and classify common web attacks like XSS, Directory Traversal, SQL injection, Command Injection and benign payloads recognition. LLMs used for fine-tuning are : BERT (encoder model), Llama (decoder model), Flan-T5 (encoder-decoder model). This is one of the first comparative evaluations of BERT, Llama, and Flan-T5 fine-tuned on real-world web attack payloads. We used several fine-tuning techniques including Quantization, Low-Rank Adaptation, Supervised Fine-Tuning or freezing the weights of the LLMs layers and gradually adding fully connected layers. For each of the LLMs, we obtained an accuracy rate of over 98% after fine-tuning. Since we were working with unbalanced dataset, we also evaluated models using metrics such as Balanced Accuracy, Geometric Mean, Matthews Correlation Coefficient, Fowlkes-Mallows Index. The fine-tuned BERT and Flan-T5 models proved to be more robust to unbalanced data. Evaluation with the confusion matrix shows that fine-tuned models correctly classify web application attacks. This confirms that fine-tuning basic models is effective for specific cybersecurity tasks.

[R0603] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE Conference on Dependable and Secure Computing (DSC) | type=CONF
TITLE: Code as a Weapon: Generating Malware with Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Fault tolerance; Codes; Limiting; Large language models; Weapons; Information security; Malware; Prompt engineering; Cyberattack; large language model; malware generation; code generation; attack simulation; MITRE ATT&CK
ABSTRACT: In recent years, information security has emerged as one of the most critical challenges in the digital era. The misuse of generative artificial intelligence, the rapid mutation of malware, and the increasing sophistication of cyberattack techniques pose significant threats to cybersecurity defense systems. In response to this continuously evolving threat landscape, proactive defense strategies are attracting considerable attention. These strategies aim to enhance system resilience and validate defense mechanisms by predicting, identifying, and preventing potential attacks. Current approaches, such as red team exercises and Breach and Attack Simulation (BAS), play a critical role in assisting defenders in identifying system vulnerabilities and validating defense mechanisms. However, most attack scenarios depend heavily on existing tools or pre-defined scripts written by experts, limiting their ability to counter novel threats. Therefore, we propose a malware generation framework based on large language models MalGene. By combining prompt engineering and MITRE ATT&CK framework, our framework is capable of generating attacks samples with complete lifecycle automatically. Experimental results show that all of the samples generated by MalGene are executable, and 9.72 % were labeled as suspicious software by antivirus software, demonstrating a certain level of evasiveness. Through case studies, we further demonstrate that MalGene can produce samples corresponding to the ATT&CK framework, showing its potential for application in simulation and proactive defense exercises.

[R0604] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE 49th Annual Computers, Software, and Applications Conference (COMPSAC) | type=CONF
TITLE: SmartTinkerer: Bridging Smart Home Testing Gaps with LLM, Digital Twin & Reinforcement Learning
KEYWORDS: Software testing; Translation; Large language models; Reinforcement learning; Smart homes; Software; Filling; Digital twins; Internet of Things; Security; Large Language Model (LLM); Digital Twins; Software Testing; Reinforcement Learning; Internet of Things (IoT)
ABSTRACT: There has been a significant rise in the use of Internet of Things (IoT) devices in recent years, but along with the increase in usage, there’s also a rise in security vulnerabilities in the IoT software. The security vulnerabilities are difficult to find, and even more difficult to exploit dynamically. The software technologies, particularly used in the Samsung SmartThings environment, have a huge gap in terms of deprecated language usage, having a good testing environment and a security vulnerability scanning. This paper introduces a new tool, SmartTinkerer , filling these gaps by employing LLMs to translate deprecated language to new APIs, creating a digital twin simulating a virtual smart home device system for a testing environment and using reinforcement learning on the digital simulation to better scan for vulnerabilities.

[R0605] year=2026 | venue=2026 10th International Conference on Electronic Information Technology and Computer Engineering (EITCE) | type=CONF
TITLE: LLM-Agent Assisted Black-Box Detection of IDOR/BOLA via Multi-Role Differential Validation
KEYWORDS: Authorization; Application programming interfaces; Large language models; Security; Testing; Barium; Signal detection; Manuals; Modeling; Planning; IDOR; BOLA; broken object-level authorization; Web API security; black-box testing; LLM agent; differential validation
ABSTRACT: Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) and Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA) remain the highest-ranked risk in the OWASP API Security Top 10 [1], yet they resist automated discovery because the verdict depends on per-object ownership semantics that traditional DAST scanners and REST API fuzzers cannot recover from HTTP traffic alone. Recent Large Language Model (LLM) agents can interpret endpoint paths, parameter names, and response fields [7], but using an LLM as the vulnerability oracle introduces hallucination-driven false positives. AUTHSENTRY is proposed as an LLM-agent-assisted black-box framework that decouples semantic reasoning from vulnerability decision-making: an LLM agent extracts authorization-sensitive endpoints, object identifiers, ownership hints, and structured cross-user test plans, while a deterministic multi-role differential validator issues owner, non-owner, and cross-user replays and adjudicates the verdict over status codes, owner indicators, sensitive fields, denial signals, and response similarity. Using the supplied reproducible IDOR/BOLA artifact, AUTHSENTRY is evaluated on a local FastAPI target containing two users, ten authorization-sensitive endpoints, and five seeded vulnerabilities. The experiment scans all endpoints and obtains TP=5, FP=0, FN=0, and TN=5, yielding 100% precision, 100% recall, and 100% F1 on the controlled artifact while preserving a replay log for every decision.

[R0606] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=JOUR
TITLE: Testing the Robustness of IoT Messaging Middleware: A Case Study Using Rules and Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Internet of Things; Modeling; Testing; Robustness; Large language models; Protocols; Middleware; Security; Libraries; Conferences; Fault injection; Internet of Things; large language models; messaging middleware; robustness testing; software security; software testing
ABSTRACT: The Internet of Things (IoT) allows the inter-connection of objects, equipped with specialized hardware and software, in complex networked systems designed to fulfill business goals. IoT is frequently supported by asynchronous messaging middleware, which must operate correctly even in the presence of invalid or malicious data. While prior research has examined the robustness of asynchronous applications, the assessment of the corresponding middleware is relatively scarce with known studies pointing it out as being mostly robust, at least when using traditional techniques. In this paper, we assess the robustness of messaging middleware (i.e., ActiveMQ Classic 6.1.5, RabbitMQ 4.0.5) designed for IoT systems and that implement typical IoT communication protocols (i.e., MQTT 3.1.1, STOMP 1.2, AMQP 1.0) in the following two ways: i) we generate test cases using traditional robustness testing techniques; and ii) we use three off-the-shelf large language models (i.e., gemma3_12b, qwen2.5-coder_32b, mistral-small3.2) to generate test cases by using three distinct prompts. Results show that message brokers are highly robust, whereas client libraries are the weakest link, failing more often with invalid inputs, typically by forwarding known invalid messages to lower layers. The models increased fault diversity, especially for security-oriented faults, but did not consistently outperform rule-based testing, matching it at an equal test budget and surpassing it in 3 of 6 scenarios only when combining multiple roles, i.e., using a larger cumulative number of tests.

[R0607] year=2026 | venue=2026 Information Technology Scientific Research Conference (IIT.SRC) | type=CONF
TITLE: Detection of Malicious HTTP Traffic Using Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Labeling; HTTP; Measurement; Optimization; Training; Equations; Multi label classification; Tuning; Large language models; large language models; HTTP traffic; detection of malicious activity
ABSTRACT: Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) traffic constitutes the most prevalent traffic type on the modern internet and, consequently, serves as the primary vector for many diverse cyberattacks. Accurately identifying and categorizing these threats is essential for maintaining robust network security. In this paper we evaluate the applicability of large language models (LLM) for multi-label classification of HTTP traffic. The presented solution follows two approaches in categorizing this type of traffic and compares their efficacy when applied to public datasets and real production traffic. While in the first part of this paper we present an approach that utilizes a fine-tuned LLM with a decoder-only architecture, in the second part of this paper we focus on the automated optimization of prompts for generative LLM for achieving the same goals. These methodologies will serve as the baseline for future work, in which we intend to explore the applicability of LLM agents as a part of this classification pipeline.

[R0608] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Computer, Data Sciences and Applications (ACDSA) | type=CONF
TITLE: Transforming Cybersecurity Research: AI as Our Research Assistant in Academia
KEYWORDS: Payloads; Protocols; Communication systems; Computer networks; HTTP; Internet; Radio access networks; Regional area networks; Storage area networks; Artificial intelligence; Artificial Intelligence; Cybersecurity Research; Large Language Models
ABSTRACT: Artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models (LLMs), is quickly transforming the field of academic cybersecurity research. It is no longer just a tool for basic automation; instead, AI is becoming an active partner in the research process. It assists researchers in analyzing large volumes of data, identifying security weaknesses, and gathering and organizing threat information more efficiently than ever before. This paper examines how the collaboration between AI and cybersecurity researchers is developing within academic environments, focusing on areas where AI has shown significant value, such as finding software vulnerabilities, analyzing malicious software, and responding to security incidents. The discussion draws from practical experiences at top research organizations and global partnerships, emphasizing the need for clear and open processes, the ability to understand AI decisions, protection of personal information, and strong ethical guidelines. To focus our study, we examine two main questions: one about how AI is helping researchers, and the other about the possibility of AI becoming more independent in conducting research. While AI offers clear benefits in terms of efficiency and new findings, our analysis also highlights the risks of depending too much on AI results and stresses the continued necessity of thorough human review. Our goal is to assist academic institutions that want to use AI in their work, ensuring that cybersecurity research progresses while maintaining high ethical and scholarly standards.

[R0609] year=2024 | venue=2024 International Conference on Electrical, Computer and Energy Technologies (ICECET | type=CONF
TITLE: An Extended Review: LLM Prompt Engineering in Cyber Defense
KEYWORDS: Industries; Training; Analytical models; Reviews; Chatbots; Market research; Prompt engineering; Artificial intelligence; Computer security; Monitoring; Artificial Intelligence; Machine Learning; Natural Language Processing; Generative AI; Large Language Models; Prompt Engineering; ChatGPT; Cyber-Defense; Cybersecurity; Vulnerability Management
ABSTRACT: The launch of ChatGPT in November 2022 marked a significant advancement in the field of artificial intelligence, particularly in the realm of generative AI (GAI). ChatGPT is based on the Large Language Model (LLM). Use of AI in Cybersecurity can prove to be beneficial as the security analysts are employing AI models for improved detection of threats and quicker response to incidents. The interaction with LLMs needs to be skillfully and tactfully created to get precise and concise response. This technique of crafting queries to interact with the LLM is called prompt engineering. Prompt engineering in vulnerability detection and management is a new trend in the industry to manage cybersecurity threats and weaknesses proactively and effectively. This paper presents an extensive review of the field of prompt engineering in cybersecurity. It is primarily based on a comprehensive analysis of existing literature, encompassing a wide range of sources to provide a thorough overview of the current state and advancements in AI. The review delves into various aspects of prompt engineering, synthesizing key findings and theories from a multitude of scholarly articles and industry reports. This approach ensures a holistic understanding of the AI models including LLMs and how generative AI, in terms of prompt engineering, can be used for cyber-defense.

[R0610] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE World Forum on Public Safety Technology (WF-PST) | type=CONF
TITLE: ThreatGPT: An Agentic AI Framework for Enhancing Public Safety Through Threat Modeling
KEYWORDS: Threat modeling; Agentic AI; Urban areas; Training data; NIST; Traffic control; Public security; Robustness; Few shot learning; Computer security; GPT; Threat Modeling; GEMINI; Public Safety; Agentic AI; STRIDE; MITRE ATT&CK; CVE; NIST; NVD
ABSTRACT: As our cities and communities become smarter, the systems that keep us safe, such as traffic control centers, emergency response networks, and public transportation, also become more complex. With this complexity comes a greater risk of security threats that can affect not just machines but real people's lives. To address this challenge, we present ThreatGPT, an agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI) assistant built to help people whether they are engineers, safety officers, or policy makers to understand and analyze threats in public safety systems. Instead of requiring deep cybersecurity expertise, it allows users to simply describe the components of a system they are concerned about, such as login systems, data storage, or communication networks. Then, with the click of a button, users can choose how they want the system to be analyzed by using popular frameworks such as STRIDE, MITRE ATT&CK, CVE reports, NIST, or CISA. ThreatGPT is unique because it does not just provide threat information, but rather it acts like a knowledgeable partner. Using few-shot learning, the AI learns from examples and generates relevant smart threat models. It can highlight what might go wrong, how attackers could take advantage, and what can be done to prevent harm. Whether securing a city's infrastructure or a local health service, this tool adapts to users' needs. In simple terms, ThreatGPT brings together AI and human judgment to make our public systems safer. It is designed not just to analyze threats, but to empower people to understand and act on them, faster, smarter, and with more confidence.

[R0611] year=2026 | venue=2026 13th International Conference on Electrical, Electronic and Computing Engineering (IcETRAN) | type=CONF
TITLE: Metaheuristic Optimization of Boosting and Hybrid Machine Learning Models for IoT Intrusion Detection: A Review
KEYWORDS: Internet of Things; Modeling; Optimization; Intrusion detection; Printing; Algorithms; Metaheuristics; Tuning; Machine learning; Feature extraction; IoT; cyber security; intrusion detection; AI; metaheuristics
ABSTRACT: The rapid expansion of Internet of Things (IoT) infrastructures has significantly increased the attack surface of modern digital ecosystems. Due to constrained computational resources and heterogeneous device architectures, traditional intrusion detection mechanisms are often inadequate for IoT environments. Machine learning techniques have emerged as efficient solutions for intelligent intrusion detection systems, while metaheuristic optimization algorithms have demonstrated substantial improvements in feature selection and hyperparameter tuning processes. This paper provides a comprehensive review of metaheuristicoptimized machine learning approaches for IoT intrusion detection. Existing methods are systematically categorized based on optimization strategy, learning architecture, and application domain. Particular emphasis is placed on boosting-based models, deep learning frameworks, and hybrid multi-level optimization strategies. Furthermore, commonly used datasets, evaluation methodologies, and performance trends are analyzed to identify current research directions and limitations. The study highlights emerging trends, including domain-specific IoT applications and hybrid optimization frameworks, while identifying open challenges related to benchmarking, deployment efficiency, and generalization across heterogeneous IoT scenarios.

[R0612] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (CNS) | type=CONF
TITLE: Penetration Testing of Agentic AI: A Longitudinal Security Analysis Across Models and Frameworks
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Tools; Cells (biology); Codes; Large language models; Testing; Security; Internet; Printing; Search engines; Agentic AI; Security; Penetration Testing; Prompt Injection; LLM Safety
ABSTRACT: Agentic AI introduces security vulnerabilities that traditional LLM safeguards fail to address. Prior work (notably Unit 42 at Palo Alto Networks) showed that a single model executes attacks as an agent that it refuses in chat mode, but no study compares vulnerabilities across multiple frontier models, orchestration patterns, and model generations. We evaluate the same seven-agent university-information testbed under 13 attack scenarios on two frameworks (AutoGen swarm-handoff, CrewAI hierarchical delegation), across a 2025 study of five then-current models (single trial, hand-coded) and a 2026 rerun of each provider’s frontier successor ($n=5,650$ traces, labeled by an LLM judge validated against a 50-cell human audit at 94% agreement). Three findings stand out. First, the orchestration framework matters as much as the model: across both generations AutoGen contains more attacks than CrewAI (60.9% vs 48.3% in the rerun, non-overlapping Wilson 95% CIs), and the finding survives excluding a benign control and scoring fabricated executions as failures. Second, frontier safety training has largely closed the active-execution attacks (SSRF, SQL injection, code execution) that succeeded a year earlier, leaving system-information disclosure as the dominant residual failure. Third, provider improvements are uneven, but because a same-id control model shifts by about 5 points between the two pipelines, we treat cross-generation deltas as testbed-specific rather than definitive rankings. We also name and quantify hallucinated compliance, where a model narrates a tool result that was never invoked: rare overall (2.2% of rerun cells) but concentrated in CrewAI (12 of 14 cases). We release all prompts, full traces, and a programmatic detector.

[R0613] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Intelligent Digitization of Systems and Services (IDSS) | type=CONF
TITLE: When Will the Next Attack Start? Calibrated Time-Window Prediction Using Multi-Agent LLMs
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Timing; Large language models; Windows; Retrieval augmented generation; Printing; Context; Cognition; Cognitive systems; Cyberattack; large language models; network attacks; prediction; time-window
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity is moving from reactive detection to proactive prediction, but current machine learning and LLM methods emphasize classification or coarse forecasting rather than predicting when a specific attack will start. We pose cyberattack prediction as a time-window localization problem, where the system must output an interval for the next attack with calibrated confidence. We introduce a hybrid retrieval-augmented, multi-agent LLM pipeline that converts network flow statistics into structured natural language context, retrieves similar historical traffic patterns from an embedding-indexed memory, and uses three specialized agents (threat analyst, quantitative estimator, and peer reviewer) to generate, refine, and validate predictions via a confidence-aware retry loop. On CIC-IDS-2017 and a zero-day logistics-network dataset, our system attains a Localization Score of 0.950 with 98% coverage over 50 trials per configuration, substantially outperforming single-agent baselines, especially under temporal ambiguity (96% vs. 36% coverage on the harder dataset). These results show that structured multi-agent reasoning with retrieval grounding markedly improves robustness, coverage, and uncertainty communication for operational attack time-window prediction.

[R0614] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE 50th Conference on Local Computer Networks (LCN) | type=CONF
TITLE: Designing a Maritime Cybersecurity Risk Intelligence Model with Generative AI and Real-Time Interviews
KEYWORDS: Surveys; Text analysis; Generative AI; Computational modeling; Soft sensors; Transportation; Real-time systems; Risk analysis; Interviews; Computer security; Maritime Cybersecurity; Large Language Models; Risk Intelligence; Generative AI; Real-Time Interviews
ABSTRACT: AI has disrupted all sectors, and the maritime industry is undergoing its own transformation. After reviewing 48 academic papers, the authors showed the need for a holistic and engaging cybersecurity risk analysis method and suggested a survey-based method called Cyber Risk Analysis Method for Maritime Transportation Systems (CRAMMTS). This study presents an AI-powered system for conducting risk analysis interviews with maritime entities, developed using LangChain, LangGraph and OpenAI’s GPT-4o in Python. The system uses external sources, such as Maritime Attack Database and CRAMMTS survey results, as a data source, evaluates each question/response pair in real-time, determining the necessity of follow-up questions and ensuring thorough risk coverage. The system stores the interview results, applies basic analysis, and generates recommendation statements for the entity. Retrieval augmented generation is employed to integrate current incidents and risk data into the analysis. Future expansion opportunities include automated analysis and the generation of comprehensive risk analysis documents with actionable recommendations. This approach not only aims to streamline and standardize the maritime risk analysis process but also reduce the cost barrier to cybersecurity resources using advanced language models.

[R0615] year=2025 | venue=2025 International Conference on Information Technology, Communication Ecosystem and Management (ITCEM) | type=CONF
TITLE: Research on AIGC-Based Vulnerability Detection and Repair Systems
KEYWORDS: Filtering; Filters; Protocols; Location awareness; Graphical user interfaces; Communications technology; Smart contracts; Mobile communication; HTTP; Data communication; AIGC; code vulnerabilities; automated repair; large language models; intelligent software security
ABSTRACT: Facing the core challenges of traditional code vulnerability repair, which heavily relies on expert experience, is inefficient, and difficult to scale, this paper explores a new automated repair approach based on generative artificial intelligence. The study aims to build an intelligent repair mechanism that integrates deep semantic understanding with code generation capabilities. The core of this mechanism lies in leveraging large language models to perform joint reasoning on code context and vulnerability patterns, thereby automatically synthesizing repair patches that maintain semantic consistency and functional correctness. This paper details how to embed the AIGC repair engine into an automated ‘detection-repair-verification’ loop and introduces the key technologies used to improve repair accuracy, guided by a retrieval-enhanced knowledge base of vulnerabilities. Through practical validation in the ‘ZhiHu’ system, this mechanism provides a feasible technical solution and theoretical support for the paradigm shift from ‘human-assisted repair’ to ‘AI autonomous repair’.

[R0616] year=2026 | venue=2026 2nd International Conference on Computational Intelligence Approaches and Applications (ICCIAA) | type=CONF
TITLE: AI-Enhanced BIM for Smart City SDGs: Governance and Environment Pillars
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Building information management; Tools; Standards; Large language models; Security; Printing; Urban areas; Digital twins; Urban digital twin; building information modeling; generative AI; AI integration; AI governance; LLMOps; MLOps; cybersecurity; SDG indicators; trustworthy AI
ABSTRACT: Urban digital twins (UDTs) increasingly combine building information modeling (BIM), geospatial information systems (GIS), and Internet-of-Things (IoT) sensing to support evidence-based city governance and sustainability reporting. In parallel, cities are adopting machine learning (ML) and foundation models (including large language models, LLMs) to automate indicator computation, forecasting, and decision support. Yet integrating AI into BIM-enabled UDT pipelines remains difficult due to fragmented data ecosystems, interoperability constraints, evolving regulations, and emerging security and safety risks. This paper reframes SDG-aligned UDT development as an AI integration problem. We propose a layered reference architecture for AI-enabled BIM-to-city digital twins and an end-to-end AI integration lifecycle that unifies MLOps/LLMOps practices with public-sector governance constraints. We synthesize current integration challenges in a lifecycle-oriented matrix and derive a risk–mitigation mapping grounded in recent standards and security guidance. Finally, we outline future trends agentic systems, multimodal AI, edge/on-device AI, retrieval and tool use, regulation, trustworthy AI, and evaluation/observability and discuss implications for municipal governance, sustainability decisionmaking, and building/urban-quality evaluation (e.g., daylighting, acoustics, spatial connectivity, and biophilic performance) beyond energy and carbon reporting.


